"src/src-sample-contract": {
"id": "src-sample-contract",
"title": "Sample contract chapter: Agreement and Estoppel",
"module": "contract",
"kind": "pdf",
"pages": 3,
"chars": 1147,
"parts": 1,
"createdAt": "2026-01-10T09:00:00Z"
},
"srctext/src-sample-contract_0": {
"sid": "src-sample-contract",
"n": 0,
"text": "Agreement\n\nAn offer is a statement of the terms on which the offeror is willing to be bound. In Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball Co the advert was treated as an offer to the whole world because it showed a clear intention to be bound. By contrast, in Partridge v Crittenden an advert for birds was only an invitation to treat. Goods on display in a shop window are an invitation to treat, as Fisher v Bell shows.\fAcceptance\n\nAcceptance must mirror the offer. A counter-offer destroys the original offer, as in Hyde v Wrench. Silence cannot normally amount to acceptance, which is the point of Felthouse v Bindley. Under the postal rule an acceptance takes effect when posted, following Adams v Lindsell.\fPromissory estoppel\n\nWhere a promise not to enforce strict legal rights is relied on, the promisor may be prevented from going back on it. The doctrine grew from Hughes v Metropolitan Railway and was restated in Central London Property Trust v High Trees House. Combe v Combe confirms that promissory estoppel is a shield and not a sword. Terms may be implied from custom, as in Hutton v Warren, where a local custom gave the tenant an allowance."
},
"src/src-sample-tort": {
"id": "src-sample-tort",
"title": "Sample tort chapter: Negligence and Causation",
"module": "tort",
"kind": "pdf",
"pages": 2,
"chars": 597,
"parts": 1,
"createdAt": "2026-01-10T09:00:00Z"
},
"srctext/src-sample-tort_0": {
"sid": "src-sample-tort",
"n": 0,
"text": "Duty of care\n\nDonoghue v Stevenson established that a manufacturer owes a duty to the ultimate consumer. In novel situations the courts reason by analogy with decided cases, as explained in Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire.\fCausation\n\nThe claimant must show that the breach caused the damage. In Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington Hospital the claimant failed the but-for test. In Bonnington Castings v Wardlaw the dust from the guilty source made a material contribution to the pneumoconiosis. Where the science cannot separate the causes, a material contribution to the harm is enough."
},
"src/src-sample-criminal": {
"id": "src-sample-criminal",
"title": "Sample criminal chapter: Mens Rea",
"module": "criminal",
"kind": "pdf",
"pages": 2,
"chars": 272,
"parts": 1,
"createdAt": "2026-01-10T09:00:00Z"
},
"srctext/src-sample-criminal_0": {
"sid": "src-sample-criminal",
"n": 0,
"text": "Intention\n\nOblique intention is found where death or serious injury was a virtual certainty and the defendant appreciated that, following R v Woollin.\fRecklessness\n\nSubjective recklessness requires awareness of a risk which it was unreasonable to take, as stated in R v G."
}
